```

Notes on alert event shape:

- The TA emits **two parallel naming conventions** for the same
  underlying value: a dotted `alert.*` form (e.g. `alert.test.name`,
  `alert.rule.name`, `alert.severity`, `alert.type`) and an
  underscore-flat form (e.g. `alert_test_name`, `alert_rule_name`,
  `alert_type`). Both are populated. Pick one and stay consistent
  inside a single stanza so readers can grep either way; prefer
  the dotted form because it round-trips with the DM dataset
  field names where they overlap.
- `alertState` (string: `"active"` / `"cleared"`) is the live
  status. `alert_cleared` is a boolean flag carrying the same
  signal in flat form. Filter with `alertState="active"` for
  open-alert tiles.
- `severity` is the human label (`"info"`, `"minor"`, `"major"`,
  `"critical"` per the TA). `severity_id` is the numeric form.
  `vendor_severity` is the upstream TE severity string before any
  TA mapping.
- `alert.targets{}` is a multivalue carrying the alert's affected
  targets (test endpoints, server addresses, etc.). Use
  `mvexpand alert.targets{}` if you need per-target fan-out, or
  `dc(alert.targets{})` for target-count aggregation.
- `permalink` is the deep-link URL into the TE web UI for the
  alert; `itsi_drilldown_uri` (and the camelCase
  `itsiDrilldownURI`) are deep links into ITSI for operators who
  use Splunk's correlation engine.
- `triggerTime` is the alert fire time as recorded by TE
  (separate from Splunk's `_time`); `trigger_time_formatted` is
  the human-readable rendering that the TA pre-computes.
- `eventType` distinguishes the alert family (`"ALERT"`,
  `"ALERT_CLEARED"`, etc.). When grouping per alert, prefer
  `dc(alertId)` over `count` so a fire+clear pair counts as one
  alert, not two events.
- `testId` / `testName` / `testType` (and the dotted
  `alert.test.name`) tie the alert back to a specific synthetic
  test. Prefer explicit topology bindings. If a deployment encodes site names
  in `testName`, treat those values as deployment configuration rather than
  hard-coding them in generic searches.

